{"type":"message","id":"bfe3ab83","parentId":"91f54673","timestamp":"2026-05-16T00:03:37.974Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Interesting - we see \"Pushing to github.com:blocklee/my-openclaw.git\" but then SIGTERM. So the push starts but gets killed before completing.\n\nWait - this looks like the cron might be killing long-running processes. Or maybe the exec tool itself has a timeout. Let me check if there are other processes or if this is a system-level issue.\n\nActually, looking at this pattern - each command that runs git push gets killed with SIGTERM. This suggests the process management is killing it. Let me try running the push in background mode.\n\nActually, let me think about this differently. The session says \"Command still running\" which means it started. Then when I poll, it says \"Process exited with signal SIGTERM\". \n\nThe SIGTERM is being sent to the process, which could be coming from the cron system itself enforcing a timeout, or it might be a memory or resource constraint. Let me try pushing just the committed changes instead of dealing with these large file issues.\n\nWait - I just realized: the git add and commit worked fine, but the push keeps getting killed. And now there are new uncommitted changes (the SQLite files). Let me check if the push actually succeeded before it got killed. Let me try using the `process` tool to manage the push in the background instead of letting it get terminated.\n\nActually, thinking about this more carefully: maybe the push did succeed, and the SIGTERM is just a side effect of how the timeout was implemented. The `git push -v` output shows \"Pushing to github.com:blocklee/my-openclaw.git\" which suggests it started. Since ls-remote works fine, the SSH connection should be functional. But the push is getting killed before producing more output - which could mean either the connection is hanging on the actual data transfer, or the process is being killed by the syste