{"type":"message","id":"1c5d5550","parentId":"ea106fac","timestamp":"2026-05-16T00:05:00.484Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Interesting! Exit code 124 means `timeout` killed it. But we also see `git pack-objects` started and then it got killed. The SSH connection works fine (`git-receive-pack` was called). The problem seems to be that `pack-objects` is very slow.\n\nWait, but what was it packing? The commit only had a few files added. Unless... let me think about the previous commits. Maybe there are a lot of objects that need to be packed and sent?\n\nActually, looking at the initial output from the first successful commit - it was committing a LOT of npm cache files. Those were the ones that got added in the first `git add -A`. And then the push might be trying to upload those objects.\n \nThe exit code 124 confirms `timeout` killed it after 15 seconds, not SIGTERM. The real issue is that the pack operation is taking longer than 15 seconds to complete. I should increase the timeout or use a background process with polling instead. But wait - the commit had many files added, but the remote already had those objects from previous commits, so git should only be sending new objects, not re-uploading everything.\n\nLet me try using a much longer timeout with the `--no-progress` flag to reduce output, and check the git config to see if there's compression or other settings slowing things down.\n","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"text","text":"\n\n\n"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call-767a1ade-6770-4cad-a62b-bc712afa7712","name":"exec","arguments":{"timeout":125,"command":"cd ~/.openclaw && timeout 120 git push --no-progress 2>&1; echo \"exit:$?\""}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"nvidia","model":"minimaxai/minimax-m2.7","usage":{"input":241,"output":334,"cacheRead":29376,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":29951,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timesta